  "single_vs_multi": {
    "single_agent": {
      "status": "COMPLETED",
      "answer_result": "## Methods reported in the three papers\n\n| Paper | Method | Params | mIoU | Claimed design story |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| paper_1.pdf | Method A — convolutional segmentation | 2.5M | 73.27 | Strong mid-budget convolutional baseline [paper_1.pdf, p.1] |\n| paper_2.pdf | Method B — attention segmentation | 3.2M | 76.0 | Attention gives the highest quality, at the highest cost [paper_2.pdf, p.1] |\n| paper_3.pdf | Method C — compact convolution baseline | 2.0M | 70.0 | Cheapest configuration; lower quality [paper_3.pdf, p.1] |\n\nAll three papers describe the same fixture benchmark and evaluation protocol, and all three explicitly frame their claims as a **descriptive quality/cost tradeoff only — no causal or ablation claim** [paper_1.pdf, p.1][paper_2.pdf, p.1][paper_3.pdf, p.1].\n\n## experiment.xlsx results\n\nThe workbook contains a single sheet with 3 rows and 3 columns (`model`, `params_m`, `miou`), no missing values [Analysis 34e957e8-dd44-4233-ad78-920802f6fc85]:\n\n| model | params_m | miou |\n|---|---|---|\n| A | 2.5 | 73.27 |\n| B | 3.2 | 76.0 |\n| C | 2.0 | 70.0 |\n\nAcross the full set: mean params 2.57M, mean mIoU 73.09, range 70.0–76.0 [Analysis 34e957e8-dd44-4233-ad78-920802f6fc85].\n\n## Best configuration under 3M parameters\n\nFiltering to `params_m < 3` leaves two candidates:\n\n- **A — 2.5M params, mIoU 73.27** ← best\n- C — 2.0M params, mIoU 70.0\n\n**Model A is the best configuration under the 3M budget**, beating C by 3.27 mIoU points for 0.5M additional parameters [Analysis 34e957e8-dd44-4233-ad78-920802f6fc85]. Model B is excluded: at 3.2M parameters it exceeds the budget, though it posts the highest raw mIoU (76.0) [Analysis 34e957e8-dd44-4233-ad78-920802f6fc85].\n\n## Chart\n\nA `params_m` vs `miou` comparison chart was generated from experiment.xlsx and saved as **comparison.png** in the workspace [Analysis 34e957e8-dd44-4233-ad78-920802f6fc85].\n\n## Do the results support the reported design advantages?\n\n**Largely yes, but only at the descriptive level — and this is not independent validation.**\n\nSupporting points:\n- The experiment values are **numerically identical** to the paper-reported values for all three methods (A: 2.5M/73.27; B: 3.2M/76.0; C: 2.0M/70.0) [Analysis 34e957e8-dd44-4233-ad78-920802f6fc85][paper_1.pdf, p.1][paper_2.pdf, p.1][paper_3.pdf, p.1]. The ordering and gaps confirm the reported tradeoff: A over C by 3.27 points at +0.5M params, and B over A by 2.73 points at +0.7M params — the same arithmetic stated in the papers [paper_1.pdf, p.1].\n- The quality-vs-cost pattern (attention > convolution > compact baseline, with monotonically increasing parameter count) is reproduced exactly.\n\nImportant limitations:\n- Because the workbook values match the paper values exactly rather than differing by any re-measured amount, this is a **replication of the fixture numbers, not an independent re-test**. It cannot confirm that the design choices (attention vs. convolution) *cause* the gains.\n- The papers themselves state their claims are **descriptive tradeoff only, with no causal or ablation evidence**, and the fixture provides **three configurations with no uncertainty estimates** [paper_2.pdf, p.1].\n- The dataset is a 3-row synthetic fixture, so any conclusion is limited to these three points; there is no statistical power to support generalization.\n\n**Bottom line:** Model A is the best sub-3M configuration (2.5M params, 73.27 mIoU), and experiment.xlsx is fully consistent with the three papers' reported parameter budgets and mIoU figures. The results are consistent with the reported design advantages as a *quality/cost ordering*, but they do not independently validate a causal design advantage, since the values reproduce the fixtures' own numbers and carry no uncertainty estimates.",
